//! System-linker driver for trust-cg objects.
//!
//! A [`LinkRequest`] describes one link through the host C compiler driver
//! (`cc`): the output kind (executable or shared object), position
//! independence, static and `-nostdlib` links, a shared object's soname,
//! run-time search paths and validated `-Wl,` passthrough groups. Validation
//! refuses contradictory or target-inapplicable options by name before any
//! process runs; nothing is silently dropped. Every Linux (ELF) link requests
//! a non-executable stack (`-Wl,-z,noexecstack`), and a passthrough asking
//! for an executable stack is refused. trust-cg emits no BTI landing pads, no
//! PAC instructions and no `GNU_PROPERTY_AARCH64_FEATURE_1_AND` note, so a
//! passthrough asking for a BTI or PAC link configuration (`-z force-bti`,
//! `-z pac-plt`, `-z bti-report[=<mode>]`) is refused with
//! `[TCG-LINK-BTI-PAC]` on every target. The reason differs per keyword (see
//! `bti_pac_refusal`): `force-bti` makes GNU ld and lld mark the output BTI;
//! `pac-plt` makes lld mark it PAC and GNU ld add authenticating PLT stubs;
//! `bti-report` marks nothing and is refused as part of a BTI link.
//!
//! [`classify_linker_stderr`] turns GNU ld (BFD), LLVM lld and Apple ld64
//! stderr into [`LinkDiagnostic`] records (undefined symbol, duplicate
//! definition, text relocation, relocation out of range, non-PIC relocation in
//! a shared object, missing library, local-exec TLS the output cannot resolve,
//! a thread-local symbol paired with a non-thread-local one). The classifier
//! reads message text; a line it does not recognize is reported as
//! [`LinkDiagnosticKind::Unclassified`] only when nothing else was recognized,
//! so a failure is never reported without a diagnostic.
//!
//! Before a shared-object link runs, [`run_link`] reads every positional
//! input that is an AArch64 ELF relocatable object and refuses a local-exec
//! TLS relocation (`R_AARCH64_TLSLE_*`) with `[TCG-LINK-TLS-MODEL]`
//! ([`local_exec_tls_refusals`]). lld refuses such a link itself, and GNU ld
//! refuses it against an undefined symbol, but GNU ld 2.42 resolves it
//! against a thread-local the shared object defines and writes a library
//! whose accesses land in the executable's TLS block. Its data and function
//! twins refuse a direct-address relocation against a preemptible symbol
//! (`[TCG-LINK-PREEMPTIBLE-DIRECT]`, [`direct_address_refusals`]: the
//! address forms, the conditional branches GNU ld and gold resolve without
//! the PLT, and the local-dynamic TLS forms; a name
//! exempt as hidden by another input or bound inside the output by the
//! linker loses its exemption under a `-Wl` option that can define, re-bind
//! or redirect it, and after the link it is refused when the image's
//! dynamic symbol table exports it with default visibility) and, for
//! objects the request says trust-cg compiled `static` or `pie`
//! ([`LinkRequest::compiled_relocation_model`]), every default-visibility
//! definition they make (`[TCG-LINK-PREEMPTIBLE-DEFINITION]`,
//! [`non_pic_definition_refusals`]): such code has inlined or folded uses
//! no relocation shows. On a non-Darwin target
//! a `-Wl` group may not name a driver-owned option in any spelling GNU ld
//! 2.42 reads as one: one or two dashes, any prefix of the long name, a
//! joined `=<value>`, the joined short forms `-o<file>`, `-h<name>` and
//! `-R<dir>`, and a bare `-G` ([`parse_wl_group`]). Nor may it name, in any
//! spelling a linker reads, an option that makes a link the linker would
//! refuse succeed (`--noinhibit-exec`, `[TCG-LINK-NOINHIBIT-EXEC]`;
//! `--no-check-sections`, `[TCG-LINK-NO-CHECK-SECTIONS]`; GNU ld's `-w`,
//! `--no-warnings`, `[TCG-LINK-NO-WARNINGS]`; the options that let an
//! undefined reference link, `[TCG-LINK-UNRESOLVED-SYMBOLS]`; LLD's
//! `--ignore-data-address-equality` and `--ignore-function-address-equality`,
//! `[TCG-LINK-COPY-RELOCATION]`; `--unique`, `[TCG-LINK-C-RUNTIME-LAYOUT]`;
//! [`fail_open_option`]), `-z rel` (`[TCG-LINK-RELOCATION-FORMAT]`), `-z
//! nocopyreloc` (`[TCG-LINK-COPY-RELOCATION]`), a `--start-lib` that no later
//! `--end-lib` closes (`[TCG-LINK-C-RUNTIME-LAYOUT]`, `unclosed_start_lib`),
//! or a one-dash piece gold or GNU ld reads as a group of short options
//! (`[TCG-LINK-OPTION-GROUP]`, `short_option_group`). Every other link with
//! such an input is written to a fresh driver-owned staging directory next
//! to the requested output. The staged image is read back and moved onto the
//! output only when it is an executable (`ET_EXEC`, or `ET_DYN` with
//! `DF_1_PIE`) or a relocatable object ([`local_exec_tls_output_refusals`]).
//! A refused image is removed with its staging directory, and a successful
//! link that wrote nothing at the staged path (for example `-Wl,--version`)
//! is refused with `[TCG-LINK-NO-OUTPUT]`; neither touches the requested
//! output.
//!
//! An object can also reach the linker without being a positional input:
//! as a `-Wl` operand (`-Wl,le.o`, also inside `--start-group`), as a `-l`
//! library (`-l:le.o`), as an archive member, through a linker script's
//! `INPUT()` or `GROUP()`, or from the C driver itself. A Linux link that is
//! not a plain executable link (a `-nostdlib` executable request whose every
//! input is a positional AArch64 ELF relocatable object without local-exec
//! or GOT-addressing uses, with no `-l` and no `-Wl` group) is therefore
//! staged and run with the linker's input trace ([`INPUT_TRACE_ARGUMENT`]): GNU ld
//! 2.42 and LLD 22 then print each file they load and each archive member
//! they take (`(archive)member` and `archive(member)` respectively; GNU ld
//! prints a thin archive's member as its own path, gold 2.42's trace names
//! it `archive(<the path it opened>)`, and GNU ld's `--gc-sections` note on
//! standard output is skipped). When the staged image
//! is not a confirmed executable or relocatable object, every AArch64 ELF
//! relocatable object the trace names is read (archive members from the
//! archive itself) and a local-exec TLS relocation in any of them refuses the
//! image with `[TCG-LINK-TLS-MODEL]`. A trace that does not show what was
//! loaded is refused with `[TCG-LINK-INPUT-TRACE]`: a line naming no readable
//! file or member (a passthrough printing to standard output, such as
//! `-Wl,--verbose` or `-Wl,-M`, or an input deleted after the link), a
//! positional object missing from it, or an AArch64
//! relocatable object that cannot be read. GCC's LTO plugin deletes the
//! objects it compiles for the link: a GNU ld or gold link whose trace names
//! one is linked again with the plugin keeping them in the stage
//! (`KEEP_LTO_TEMPORARIES_ARGUMENT`, `TMPDIR` in the stage), and that link
//! is the one checked and installed. LLD 22 compiles LLVM bitcode inputs
//! (raw or wrapped, a file or an archive member, `is_llvm_bitcode_prefix`;
//! never read as a linker script; with `--fat-lto-objects`, the
//! `.llvm.lto` bitcode an ELF relocatable input of any class, byte order
//! or machine embeds) into objects its trace does not name: a link whose
//! trace names one (bitcode, or an ELF relocatable object that is not
//! AArch64 LP64, which LLD links only through its bitcode), whose `-Wl`
//! groups name `--fat-lto-objects`, or whose LLD map places input sections
//! of a file the trace does not name (`lld_map_untraced_inputs`) runs
//! again with LLD's `--lto-obj-path` and an empty
//! `--thinlto-cache-dir=` after every argument (`lto_object_arguments`),
//! that link is the one checked and installed, and the objects it kept are
//! read as loaded inputs (`examine_lto_objects`). Compiled code not shown
//! to be read (another linker's, any object LLD did not write there in
//! full, or more files the checked link's map places that the trace does
//! not name than objects read, `examine_unread_compiled_code`) leaves a
//! static program's C runtime unknown, and refuses every image whose
//! checks must read each loaded input's uses (`[TCG-LINK-LTO-OBJECTS-UNREAD]`),
//! by name. A shared library contributes no
//! relocation to the link (the linker binds against its dynamic symbols), so
//! a shared-library input is not scanned; a local-exec reference from an
//! executable to a shared library's thread-local is refused by the linker
//! and classified ([`LinkDiagnosticKind::TlsModelMismatch`]). Both the
//! pre-link and the traced scans read `SHT_REL` as well as `SHT_RELA`
//! sections: the AArch64 psABI uses `SHT_RELA` only, but GNU ld 2.42 applies
//! an `SHT_REL` section's relocations too.
//!
//! GNU ld 2.42 also writes GOT-addressing relocations without checking
//! their window: the `ADRP` page kinds (`R_AARCH64_ADR_GOT_PAGE`,
//! `R_AARCH64_TLSGD_ADR_PAGE21`, `R_AARCH64_TLSIE_ADR_GOTTPREL_PAGE21`,
//! `R_AARCH64_TLSDESC_ADR_PAGE21`; +-4 GiB of pages) with the GOT placed
//! 8 GiB from the code, and the tiny-model kinds
//! (`R_AARCH64_TLSGD_ADR_PREL21`, `R_AARCH64_TLSIE_LD_GOTTPREL_PREL19`,
//! `R_AARCH64_TLSDESC_LD_PREL19`, `R_AARCH64_TLSDESC_ADR_PREL21`; +-1 MiB)
//! with it 1.5 MiB away. It links them and truncates the value. LLD 22
//! writes `R_AARCH64_LD64_GOTPAGE_LO15` (GCC's small-PIC `-fpic` GOT load,
//! `G(GDAT(S)) - Page(GOT)`, `0 <= X < 2^15`) truncated to 15 bits when a
//! slot lies 32 KiB or more above the page of `_GLOBAL_OFFSET_TABLE_`. Every
//! aaelf64 relocation that addresses a GOT slot relative to the place it
//! patches, and each `LDR` form that measures one from the GOT base
//! (`LD64_GOTPAGE_LO15`, `LD64_GOTOFF_LO15` and their PAuth forms), has a
//! row with its own window
//! ([`AARCH64_GOT_ADDRESSING_RELOCATIONS`], [`GotReach`]), and a defined
//! `STT_GNU_IFUNC` symbol counts as a page-window use too
//! ([`IFUNC_DEFINITION`]): its PLT or IPLT entry's `ADRP` is unchecked, and
//! a linker script can hide a static image's IPLT. A GOT-base form is
//! checked from the GOT base the image's symbol table gives
//! (`_GLOBAL_OFFSET_TABLE_`, [`got_base`]; for a stripped LLD image, LLD's
//! synthetic GOT as its map places it): every place its slot can lie
//! (the section holding the base, and each `GLOB_DAT` and `IRELATIVE`
//! target, the latter only for a symbol the image defines as an IFUNC)
//! must be within the window of the base or its page, and an image
//! whose base cannot be located (stripped, no such symbol) is not shown in
//! that window. GNU ld 2.42 and gold 2.42 refuse such a value themselves,
//! so for them (by the exact identity the option table was measured with)
//! the GOT-base windows are the linker's (`base_windows_checked_by`). A link with a positional
//! input that carries such a use is staged the same way, as is every traced
//! link, and its staged image is read back
//! ([`got_page_window_output_refusals`]); the uses are those of the
//! positional inputs and of every AArch64 ELF relocatable object the trace
//! names, read in the same pass as the local-exec uses.
//! The image does not record where each slot is, and section names do not
//! say either (a linker script can move the GOT into a section of any name),
//! so the check treats every executable byte as a possible site of an
//! instruction access (every byte of the image for a `GOTPCREL32` data word)
//! and every place a slot can lie as a possible target: all file-backed
//! allocated memory, every non-allocated section a linker script placed at
//! an address or left writable (`INFO`, `COPY`, `DSECT`, `OVERLAY`), every
//! dynamic relocation target and the GOT words the dynamic section names
//! ([`got_page_reach`]). The site is not located, so a load-literal or `ADR`
//! use is in reach only when every possible slot is within 1 MiB of every
//! code byte. An image not shown to keep every such pair in a use's window,
//! and every image without a section header table (which cannot show its
//! non-allocated sections), is removed with the staging directory and
//! refused when a positional or traced input carries such a use, or when the
//! trace does not show what was loaded (`[TCG-LINK-INPUT-TRACE]`).
//! Whatever the inputs, it is also refused when it holds GOT-addressing code
//! the linker wrote itself (`LinkerGotCode`): a PLT (`R_AARCH64_JUMP_SLOT`,
//! `DT_JMPREL` or an executable `.plt`), an IPLT (`R_AARCH64_IRELATIVE` or
//! an executable `.iplt`) or the lazy TLS descriptor trampoline
//! (`DT_TLSDESC_PLT`). A call to a PLT entry carries only
//! `R_AARCH64_CALL26`, but GNU ld 2.42 writes the entry's `adrp x16` to its
//! slot as unchecked as an `R_AARCH64_ADR_GOT_PAGE`: a library whose
//! `.got.plt` lay 8 GiB from its `.plt` was installed and its user died
//! with SIGSEGV. A slot such code is known to address (a PLT entry's
//! `R_AARCH64_JUMP_SLOT` slot, the trampoline's `DT_TLSDESC_GOT` word) that
//! no code byte reaches is an observed overflow; other such code is refused
//! conservatively. Whatever the inputs, an image whose GOT layout cannot be
//! read (its relocation tables cannot be read or name more targets than the
//! check holds, or it has more than one `PT_DYNAMIC`) is refused too
//! (`[TCG-LINK-GOT-PAGE-UNCHECKED]`): such code cannot be ruled out in it.
//! The image is checked first, so the trace is read only for an image not
//! shown in reach for some window. A use whose own slot a dynamic relocation
//! names and every possible site is out of reach of is an observed overflow
//! (`relocation-out-of-range`,
//! `[TCG-LINK-GOT-PAGE-WINDOW]`); every other use is a conservative refusal
//! (`unclassified`, `[TCG-LINK-GOT-PAGE-UNPROVEN]`). A staged image that
//! cannot be read as an ELF image is refused with
//! `[TCG-LINK-GOT-PAGE-UNCHECKED]`. No refusal touches the requested output.
//! A checked image is not moved onto an existing output that is neither a
//! regular file nor a symbolic link (a device, a FIFO, a socket or a
//! directory). A traced link that writes nothing at the staged path is
//! refused only when a positional input needs its image read back or its
//! trace names a local-exec or GOT-addressing input; otherwise nothing was installed and it
//! succeeds, as a direct link would.
//!
//! trust-cg writes each FDE's `pc_begin` (and a module's personality and
//! LSDA pointers) as a `DW_EH_PE_pcrel|sdata4` field through
//! `R_AARCH64_PREL32`, whose aaelf64 2026Q2 window is `-2^31 <= X < 2^31`.
//! GNU ld 2.42 refuses a value outside it, but gold 2.42 and LLD 22.1.6
//! apply the earlier bound `X < 2^32` and write `[2^31, 2^32)`, which every
//! unwinder reads as negative: with `.eh_frame` at `0x10000000` and `.text`
//! at `0xd0000000` both installed a program whose `backtrace()` stopped
//! after one frame. So every Linux link is staged (a plain executable link
//! without the trace), and its image's `.eh_frame` is decoded as an
//! unwinder reads it (`eh_frame_check`): each FDE's `pc_begin` with its
//! CIE's `R` encoding must give a range inside executable memory, each
//! personality and LSDA pointer must resolve into allocated memory, and the
//! `.eh_frame_hdr` binary search table must agree with the FDEs, and no live
//! FDE may be hidden from a lookup by start by another (`eh_fde_overlaps`). A
//! truncated value can also land where its pointer must lie (an FDE for a
//! function 2^32 above `tcg_pad` described `tcg_pad` through LLD and gold),
//! so a 4-byte (or 2-byte) pointer whose bits also give another file-backed
//! address that lies there too is not shown to be the one its linker was
//! given (`eh_wrap_alias`). An observed failure is `relocation-out-of-range`
//! (`[TCG-LINK-EH-FRAME-WINDOW]`); an image the check cannot complete, and
//! a pointer with such another address, are `unclassified`
//! (`[TCG-LINK-EH-FRAME-UNPROVEN]`); neither touches the requested output.
//!
//! Section names and `PT_GNU_EH_FRAME` do not locate every `.eh_frame` an
//! unwinder reads: libgcc first searches the frames a program registers
//! (`crtbeginT.o`, which `--static` links, registers its `.eh_frame` input
//! through `__register_frame_info`), which lie in the `.eh_frame` input
//! wherever a linker script put it (a renamed output section, or
//! `.rodata`), with or without `--eh-frame-hdr`. So a staged Linux link
//! always writes the driver's own map: `-Xlinker -Map=<stage>/link.map`
//! goes after every argument of the request, and the value of every map
//! file option of a `-Wl` group is replaced by that path, so the linker
//! is given no map path of the user's. The map a `-Wl` group asks for is
//! written by the driver from its own after the link, where that linker
//! would have written it (GNU ld's `%` and directory forms included), never
//! to the file the output names through any chain of symbolic links, and
//! without following a link it has not resolved (`LinkMapPlan`). A `-Wl`
//! group whose last map option asks for the map on standard output (`-M`,
//! `--print-map`, `-Map=-`) keeps it there: the driver's option then goes
//! first, and GNU ld and LLD write no map file. Every `.eh_frame` input
//! piece the map of GNU ld 2.42, gold 2.42 or LLD 22 places is decoded as
//! well, in every image
//! (`eh_frame_check_placed`). Any program can register its frames (any code
//! can call a registration function, through a linker script's alias, a
//! library or `dlsym`), and the driver does not read whether one does: an
//! image whose map was not written, cannot be read, or does not show where
//! some input lies (LLD prints its pieces from the start of their output
//! section, so it cannot place pieces that share one with anything else,
//! an in-section symbol assignment included) is refused as unproven by
//! name, whatever its kind and inputs, unless that output section is read
//! whole already (a section named `.eh_frame`, or the `.eh_frame`
//! `PT_GNU_EH_FRAME` locates). For the same reason the FDEs a lookup by
//! start confuses are read as both the `.eh_frame_hdr` table and a search
//! of the registered FDEs would read them, except in a static executable,
//! taken to register its frames. In a static executable with a live FDE
//! that no table entry locates (outside the C start files), every loaded
//! input that references a registration function must be the C driver's
//! own `crtbeginT.o`, the map must show where it registers (GNU ld's and
//! gold's input section; LLD's CIE it kept for it, or its synthetic
//! `.eh_frame`'s start), no linker script or symbol-binding option may be
//! in the link, and libgcc's walk from there (to the first zero length)
//! must reach every such FDE (`eh_registered_walks`,
//! `eh_registered_verdict`); otherwise it is unproven. An image the
//! dynamic loader cannot run is refused: one that names a program
//! interpreter without a dynamic section (`[TCG-LINK-INTERP-NO-DYNAMIC]`),
//! an `ET_EXEC` without an interpreter whose dynamic section needs shared
//! libraries or holds relocations the loader applies, and a PIE without an
//! interpreter that needs shared libraries, or holds dynamic relocations
//! and links no input shown to relocate it (`[TCG-LINK-DYNAMIC-NO-INTERP]`).
//! An image of a `--static` link that names an interpreter or needs shared
//! libraries anyway is refused too (`[TCG-LINK-STATIC-DYNAMIC]`). The IFUNC
//! applier and the static-PIE relocator are glibc's only when the image
//! starts at the C driver's start file's `_start`, as the map places it
//! (`start_entry_shown`): an entry point of the user's (`-e`, `--entry`, a
//! script's `ENTRY`, another `_start`) skips them. Whatever its kind, an
//! image is refused when the memory the kernel or `ld.so` maps for it, page
//! by page at every page it can be mapped with (the host's page or its
//! largest `p_align`, at most 64 KiB on AArch64, and each smaller page an
//! AArch64 kernel uses, 16 and 4 KiB), is not what its sections hold: an
//! `ET_DYN` image's segments out of order, a page two segments map from
//! different file pages or with permissions the earlier one lacks, zero
//! fill over bytes that are not zero, overlapping segments or sections, a
//! section loaded from other bytes, an allocated section outside every
//! segment's memory, a zero-fill section over file bytes that are not zero
//! (`[TCG-LINK-LOADED-BYTES]`, `loaded_bytes_disagreement`,
//! `page_mapping_disagreement`, and `own_loaded_disagreement` for
//! trust-cg's own sections where the map places them), when a writable
//! section of trust-cg's own objects that may hold its mutable data lies in
//! the pages of a `PT_GNU_RELRO` segment that `ld.so` or glibc's static
//! start-up makes read-only, or in a `PT_LOAD` segment that is not writable
//! (`[TCG-LINK-WRITE-PROTECTED]`, `write_protection_disagreement`, the
//! placements read from the driver's map), when an allocated section of
//! trust-cg's lies in a `PT_LOAD` without `PF_R`, or its code in one without
//! `PF_X` (`[TCG-LINK-SEGMENT-PERMISSIONS]`,
//! `segment_permission_disagreement`, one predicate with the `PF_W` rule),
//! when its `PT_TLS` does not initialize each thread's block with its
//! thread-local sections as they are linked, or trust-cg's thread-local
//! sections, local-exec accesses or symbols are not where that block holds
//! them (`[TCG-LINK-TLS-TEMPLATE]`, `tls_template_disagreement`,
//! `own_tls_disagreement`), when it holds
//! relocations in a format glibc's AArch64 port does not apply (`SHT_REL`,
//! `DT_REL`, Android's packed formats, `DT_RELR` in an image with version
//! needs and a `DT_NEEDED` entry `libc.so.*` that `ld.so` loads without a
//! `GLIBC_ABI_DT_RELR` dependency: `[TCG-LINK-RELOCATION-FORMAT]`,
//! `relocation_format_refusal`), and when the linker exited successfully
//! but reported a relocation value outside its window
//! (`[TCG-LINK-RANGE-WARNING]`, `linker_range_warnings`). An executable
//! whose entry point is 0, or lies outside its executable segments' file
//! contents, is refused as well (`[TCG-LINK-ENTRY-POINT]`,
//! `entry_point_problem`): gold and LLD write 0 for an entry symbol nothing
//! defines.
//!
//! GNU ld 2.42 also relaxes an executable's initial-exec and TLS-descriptor
//! accesses into a local-exec `MOVZ`/`MOVK` pair that holds 32 bits of
//! `TPREL` and writes a larger one truncated, without a diagnostic (gold
//! 2.42 and LLD 22 refuse). An executable image whose thread-local block
//! reaches `2^32` bytes above the thread pointer is refused
//! (`unclassified`, `[TCG-LINK-TLS-TPREL-UNPROVEN]`, `tls_tprel_diagnostics`).
//! Other links (a Darwin link without a local-exec or GOT-addressing input,
//! and a link for another operating system) write the output directly, as
//! before.
//! No forwarded value (output, input and `-L` paths, `-l` names, the soname,
//! run-time search paths) may begin with `@`, which the C driver, collect2
//! and GNU ld would expand as a response file (`[TCG-LINK-RESPONSE-FILE]`).
//! The local-exec check reads positional inputs before the link and, on
//! Linux, every traced input of a non-executable image after it; the
//! GOT-addressing check reads positional inputs and, on Linux, every traced
//! input of an image not shown in reach. A plain executable link (see above)
//! is not traced: it loads only its positional inputs, which were read
//! before the link and carry no local-exec or GOT-addressing use. It is
//! staged, and its image checked, like every other Linux link.
//! Without `-nostdlib` the C driver's own start files and libraries are
//! loaded too, and they carry GOT-page `ADRP`s (`Scrt1.o`'s
//! `R_AARCH64_ADR_GOT_PAGE` against `main` on the Linux host), so every
//! such link is traced.
//!
//! A staged image of at most 1 GiB is read back whole. A larger one is read
//! back as the ranges its checks read, at their file offsets: LLD writes a
//! sparse file as long as the gap when a linker script or `--section-start`
//! places a section far from the ELF header (128 GiB for a section at
//! `0x2000000000` in a `--no-pie` link), and reading it whole exhausted the
//! host's memory. The checks run on what was read back, each range they
//! asked for that was not is read for the next run, and a run that asks for
//! nothing more saw exactly what it would have seen in the whole file
//! (`read_large_image`). An image is refused before what its checks still
//! need is read (`unclassified`, `[TCG-LINK-IMAGE-UNCHECKED]`, naming the
//! cause and the bytes read from the file in all) when
//! the ranges its checks read, and those they still ask for, come to more
//! than 1 GiB, or when they still ask for ranges after 64 runs; neither
//! touches the requested output. Whatever an image's size, each of its two
//! layout checks (the GOT-addressing one and the `.eh_frame` one) holds at
//! most 4,194,304 of each of section headers, dynamic relocation targets
//! (every table it reads together), dynamic-section entries, `.eh_frame`
//! records, `.eh_frame_hdr` table entries and GOT-base symbol addresses;
//! takes at most 16,777,216 steps through relocation entries, symbol
//! tables and `.eh_frame` records; and copies at most 64 MiB of section
//! names and 64 MiB of dynamic-symbol names. It does not read an image
//! with more than one `PT_DYNAMIC`. An image that needs more reports that
//! check unreadable or
//! unproven, by name, and is refused (whatever the inputs for the
//! GOT-addressing check, on Linux for the `.eh_frame` one). A relocation
//! table that the dynamic section and a section header, or two section
//! headers, name alike (type, offset, size and entry size) is read once.
//!
//! Apple Silicon kills arm64 code without a valid code signature. ld64 ad-hoc
//! signs every arm64 output it writes, and [`run_link`] holds it to that: after
//! a successful arm64 Darwin link the output is read back and checked by the
//! independent verifier [`crate::macho::codesign::verify_ad_hoc_signature`].
//! An unsigned or invalidly signed output (for example one linked with
//! `-Wl,-no_adhoc_codesign`) is removed and reported as
//! [`LinkError::CodeSignature`]. x86_64 Darwin outputs are not signed by ld64
//! by default and are not checked.
//!
//! After a successful arm64 Darwin link, [`record_darwin_linked_image`]
//! writes the linked-image evidence record next to the output
//! (`<output>.linked-image.json`, [`crate::macho::linked_image`]): every
//! input object's relocations (outside the linker-consumed
//! `__LD,__compact_unwind`) replayed against the image, its fixups,
//! import ordinals (against libSystem's SDK stub and any supplied export
//! lists) and read-only-after-fixup placement bound. A disagreement removes
//! the output. Every [`run_link`] first removes a record an earlier link
//! left next to the output, so a record never describes another image.

use std::ffi::OsString;
use std::fmt;
use std::path::{Path, PathBuf};
